carlos-emr (2026.09.0~snapshot22) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Saving a provider's encounter-note template (Administration > Provider
    Templates) no longer trips the web application firewall on ordinary
    clinical prose. The template body is exempted per argument on its own
    route, on the same terms as the other free-text fields; the template name
    and every other argument stay inspected, and a probe injection is still
    blocked.
  * Two browser checks now guard the chart print and the free-text saves
    through the packaged front door. One prints a chart whose note carries
    each phrase the firewall is known to misread, once per print selection,
    and requires a PDF back rather than any 200; the other replays a
    consultation request and the patient's Alert and Notes with the same
    phrases. Both prove something only on a packaged install, and the
    validation runbook says how to run them and what they write.
  * The packaging README now describes the encounter-form firewall coverage
    as it is -- generated per form, with the two cell groups that remain
    inspected named -- instead of calling the forms uncovered.

 -- CARLOS EMR Maintainers <michael@maplecreekmedical.ca>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

carlos-emr (2026.09.0~snapshot21) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Searching for an allergy and clicking a result now records it. The allergy
    page loads the picker's results over AJAX and lifts only the results
    element out of the response, so the script block that defines the click
    handler never arrived with them: every click died with "submitAddReaction
    is not defined" and a second error from a delegated handler still parsing a
    query string off an href that no longer has one. The page now carries the
    handler itself. The search POST was always a clean 200, which is why this
    looked like a working page until a result was clicked.
  * Prescribing now warns about allergies that were typed rather than picked
    out of the drug reference. DrugRef checked an allergy against the drug only
    when the allergy carried a reference category, and skipped category 0 -- the
    one CARLOS writes for every "Custom Allergy" and the one the demonstration
    dataset seeds its PENICILLINS allergies with. Prescribing amoxicillin to a
    patient recorded as allergic to PENICILLINS produced no alert and no error.
    A typed allergen name is now resolved against the drug reference by exact
    name and checked as whichever categories carry it; a name the reference does
    not know is reported as unchecked rather than as no allergy. This half of
    the fix lives in DrugRef, so it needs carlos-emr-drugref built at or past
    the revision in debian/drugref.pin, which this upload moves.

  * Clinical prose in an encounter note no longer trips the web application
    firewall. The note body travels under four parameter names on the note
    route -- the serialized chart form, the draft autosave, the save that runs
    when unsaved text is left for another note, and the CPP editor -- and only
    the last was exempt from the rule groups that read prose as an attack, so
    a note containing something as ordinary as a pasted link with
    "&cmd" in its query string, or one that simply begins with a pasted PACS
    link on an internal IP address, was answered 403 at the front door. That broke
    the note save, silently stopped its autosave, and made saving a Social
    History or other CPP item report "403 ... your session has expired" on an
    item that had in fact already saved. Exclusion 1010 now covers every
    clinician free-text argument on that route, each one individually and only
    on POST, including the save that runs when a note with unsaved text is
    left for another note or for a new one; the WAF still blocks a probe
    injection.
  * That same save-on-switch no longer cuts an encounter note off at its first
    "&" -- "H&P", or a pasted link with "&cmd=" in it, arrived truncated on the
    server -- and no longer turns a "+" into a space. The same cut-off is fixed
    on the patient page's custom telephone-encounter reason and the document
    description templates.
  * The same firewall false positive is closed on the rest of the free-text
    clinical fields: consultation requests, ticklers, prescriptions and
    allergies, preventions, document and lab comments, HRM, messenger and
    patient email, appointment reasons and notes, master-record notes and
    alerts, billing comments, fax cover comments and program notes. Each is
    exempted per argument on its own route; the firewall still inspects every
    other argument, keeps its script-injection rules on all of them, and still
    blocks a probe injection. The four fields whose
    names carry a row number (measurement comments, manual lab notes, contact
    notes, waiting-list notes) are exempted by an anchored name pattern
    instead, since the firewall cannot list them. The encounter forms (Rourke,
    antenatal, mental health, palliative care and the rest) are covered by a
    generated per-form list of their narrative cells, keyed on each form's
    save route and form class; a regression test keeps that list in step
    with the form pages. Two groups of cells are left inspected on purpose,
    because the only way to exempt them would apply to every route: the
    Vascular Tracker's bracketed cell names, which the firewall cannot list
    per route (and whose form cannot be submitted on this line anyway, see
    below), and the growth-chart and chart-checklist per-row cells, whose
    generic names (comment_<n>) a prescription field shares, so exempting
    them globally would weaken an unrelated route. The Rh-injection page
    (form/addRhInjection.jsp) posts its
    typed "other" reason as reason and reasonOtherText to the prevention route,
    and both are exempted there.
  * Reading a measurement definition file (the Vascular Tracker's, or one
    given to the measurement-type import) no longer fails on its validation
    rules, which were bound to an untyped list. The Vascular Tracker form
    itself remains unusable on this line for reasons unrelated to the
    firewall (its shortcut, setup and save actions were never finished for
    the current framework); that is a form migration, not part of this
    change, and is described in docs/ui-tests/deb-install-validation.md.
  * Opening an encounter form from the chart for a patient who has never had
    that form no longer fails with an error page: the shortcut now opens a
    new form. The Discharge Summary also opens at a clinic that does not use
    programs.
  * Saving an encounter form validates it against that form's own measurement
    definitions. The save read a process-wide list that holds whichever
    definition file was read last anywhere on the server, so a form saved
    while another provider was opening a different form could be checked
    against the other form's rules. The save and the setup step now each read
    the file they were given.
  * Leaving a note with unsaved text for another note, or for a new one, now
    shows the saved note in place. The refresh that runs after that save never
    received the note text, so the note came back as an empty box until the
    chart was reloaded, and for a brand-new note the refresh then failed on an
    element it looked up under the wrong name. Reopening a saved note for
    editing also escapes the text it puts into the editor, so a note holding
    something like "</textarea>" cannot become markup when the next clinician
    opens it. Both script-built note boxes now carry an accessible label.
  * Editing a patient's note or date on a waiting list saves again. The page
    named its row fields plainly, so the update's row selectors came out as
    "waitingListBean[undefined]" and every update fell through to a
    re-numbering of the list; the fields now carry their row index and their
    current values, as the update expects. An update posted without a usable
    list id, or a row edit missing its patient number or date, is now refused
    rather than run against an empty id or quietly turned into a re-numbering.
  * The legacy encounter-note entry view, the note history popup, the legacy
    tickler list, the prescription record and reason comments, and the
    demographic alert and notes boxes now encode the text they put back on
    the page. With the firewall no longer reading that note as a
    script attack, the application's own output encoding is what keeps a
    stored "</textarea><script>" from running on the next open of that view.
